      "textEn": "325\n\nBecause Arjuna's marriage with Subhadra was as difficult a feat as Krishna\nbringing Devaki's sons back from the mortal world, because Balram himself was opposing Arjuna's marriage to Subhadra. Sreesukha said,\nArjun, the Lord, after visiting the pilgrimage sites. Therefore, the one who is the light, the mother, and the son, is the self. 2. Duryodhana, Rama does not serve him, nor does he serve him. Tallipsu: Having become a Yati, he went to the door of Tridandi. 3. Meaning of the word\n\nShri-Shuka said—Shukadeva Goswami said; Arjuna—Arjuna; Tirtha—of holy places; Yatrayam—while traveling;\nParyatan—while wandering; Avanim—Earth; Prabhasam—to the light; Ashrunot—to hear;\nMatuleeyam—maternal uncle's daughter; Sahah—He; Atmanah—your own; Duryodhana—to Duryodhana; Ramah—Rama; Tam—to him;\nDasyati—wants to give; Iti—thus; Na—not; Cha—and; Apara—any other; Tat—his; Lipsuha—to obtain\nDesirous; He—He, Arjuna; Yati—Sannyasi (ascetic); Bhutva—Having become; Tridandi—Holding the staff; Dwarkam—Dwarka; Agat—Went. Shukdev Goswami said: While traveling far and wide and visiting various pilgrimage sites,\nArjun came to Prabhas. There, they heard that Balram wants to marry his maternal uncle's daughter\nto Duryodhana, and no one supports his plan. Arjun\nWanted to marry her himself, therefore he, adorned with the trident, disguised himself as a sanyasi\nand went to Dwarka. Meaning: Even though Arjun's plan to get Subhadra as his wife seemed against tradition,\nhe was going to do so only out of encouragement. In fact, Lord Krishna himself was their main co-\nplotter. In Dwarka, most of the members of the dynasty, especially Vasudev, were saddened by the proposal to give his beloved daughter\nDuryodhana. There, the self-interest seeker is satisfied. He was honored by the assembly, and he was also pleased with Rama. Lexicon"
