      "textEn": "326\n\nTatra—there; Vai—indeed; Varshan—of the rainy season; Masan—months; Avatisit—remained; Swa—own; Artha—purpose;\nSadhakah—trying to achieve; Pauraih—by the people of the city; Sabhajitah—honored; Ajakshnam—continuously;\nRamena—by Balarama; Ajanata—unknown; Cha—and; Saha—He. For the purpose they remained there for the whole of the rainy season. Balram and other residents of the city, without recognizing him, honored and welcomed him in every way. Once, invited by the host. With reverence, he ate the alms, and with strength, he ate the fish. 5. Meaning of the word.\n\nOnce—once; Griham—at one's (Balarama's) home; Aaniya—by bringing; Atithiyan—as a guest; Nimantranai—by inviting; Tam—to that (Arjuna); Shraddhayan—with faith; Upahritam—by offering; Bhahisakhyam—food; Balan—by Balarama; Bubhuje—ate; Kil—indeed. One day Balram, brought them to his house as a guest and Arjun respectfully accepted the food offered by Balram.\nMeaning: The explanation presented by Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti reveals that Arjuna, in the role of a renunciant,\ncompleted the Chaturmas Vrata (four-month vow) during the rainy season, and after that, he could now accept the common invitation of householders. Therefore, at that time, behind going to Balram's house,\nno one had any doubt about the unusual opinion. There, seeing the great girl, the brave one. He who is filled with love, his mind is filled with emotion. 6. Meaning of the word\n\nSah - He; Apashyat - Saw; Tatra - There; Mahatim - Wonderful; Kanyam - Girl; Veera - To the heroes; Manah - To the mind; Haraam - Attracted; Preeti - Pleasantly; Utpulah - Bloomed; Ikshanah - His eyes; Tasyaam - On him; Bhaav - By emotion; Kshubdham -\nDisturbed; Manah - Mind; Dadhe - Held, applied. There she saw the wonderful maiden Subhadra, who was enchanting the heroes. Their eyes\nOpened with joy, remained wide open, their mind became disturbed and absorbed in the same thoughts\n\nWent. The snake, having seen the cobra, is the heart of the women.\nHappy, with a burning desire, in that heart, momentarily. 7."
